{
  "dom_id": "step-q115876",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{\u2032}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$",
